From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:09:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 82/90] fix(witness): keep an unanswered publication probe out
 of the lost-work finding (gcp-9ql4)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Gate 5 of the polecat worktree reaper — "do these commits exist anywhere
other than this directory" — collapsed three distinct outcomes into one.
`CONTAINS` was blanked whenever `classify_outcome` returned anything but
`ok`, so a probe that timed out, errored, or was never run at all because
the budget was already spent became byte-identical to "the probe ran and
found no containing remote ref". The `-z "$CONTAINS"` test below could not
tell them apart and reported the confirmed-negative wording: "the commits
here exist nowhere else; kept for the witness to salvage".

That is a manufactured lost-work finding, and it fires precisely at the
budget tail — which gcp-ac59's rotating cursor moves every cycle, so over
successive runs it can point at a different healthy worktree each time.
Confirmed in gascity on 2026-09-06: one worktree, same HEAD, no intervening
change, reported `worktree_reap_pending` with "HEAD published on
origin/edge-integration,..." at 21:24 and `worktree_unpublished_kept` at
23:39 with `budget_remaining: 0`. Acting on it means the witness committing
and force-pushing already-merged commits from a detached HEAD on the
strength of a probe that never executed, and it pins the worktree against
reaping forever the way gcp-0u14's retry loop did. Same defect class as the
merge-base 1-vs-128 collapse gcp-5ddt hardened in mol-witness-patrol.

Both halves of the probe now classify their own outcome and carry an
explicit `PUBLISHED` state. Only `ok` with a non-empty containing-ref list
authorises the reap, and only `ok` with an empty one earns
`worktree_unpublished_kept`. A `timeout`/`failed` outcome (including a HEAD
that reads back empty) records the new `worktree_publication_unconfirmed`,
matching the existing `worktree_owner_unconfirmed` vocabulary; a `skipped`
outcome is the run's own clock, so it records `worktree_budget_truncated`
with `budget_spent_before_publication_probe`/`_check` and rolls the resume
cursor back to the last candidate that actually got a decision — the same
never-inspected handling the roster and git-status truncations already use.
Either way the worktree is kept and the next cycle re-checks.

Validation: the reap suite gains a five-case regression test driving the
errored, budget-cut-short and unreadable-HEAD paths against a worktree whose
content IS published, plus a control that the same worktree is still reaped
once both probes answer and a case holding the confirmed negative. Verified
RED against the pre-fix script ("a publication probe that ERRORED was
reported as proof the commits exist nowhere else") and GREEN after. Full
suite, gastown pack assets, prompt/formula drift, no-bare-bd, shellcheck -x,
go vet, golangci-lint, validate_registry and go test all pass.

The budget-`skipped` variant is handled but not driven from a test: it needs
the run's clock to cross a second boundary inside a bounded call that still
succeeds, which no test can time deterministically — the same reason the
other `budget_spent_before_*` reasons have no dedicated case.

The witness prompt and the mol-witness-patrol event table both documented
`worktree_unpublished_kept` as a salvage-now signal, so both are updated to
narrow it to the confirmed case and to say plainly that
`worktree_publication_unconfirmed` is not one.
